```java
public class Solution {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // If n is 0, return false
        if (n == 0) return false;

        // While n is divisible by 3, keep dividing it
        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3;
        }

        // If after the loop n is 1, then it's a power of three
        return n == 1;
    }
}
```

This solution works by continuously dividing `n` by 3 as long as it is divisible by 3. If after all divisions `n` becomes 1, then `n` was a power of three (specifically 3^x for some integer x). If `n` is anything other than 1 after the loop, it is not a power of three.

The key insight here is that if `n` is a power of three, then its prime factorization will only contain the prime factor 3, and the exponent of 3 in the factorization will be the power to which 3 is raised.

The constraints `-231 <= n <= 231 - 1` are considered, but this solution will work within these bounds without issues.

This solution is efficient and has a time complexity of O(log3(n)), which is quite efficient for this type of problem.

